<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Novellara</h1><p class="st-meta">1371 CE – 1737 CE (approx.) · capital Novellara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny Emilian county ruled for over three centuries by a cadet branch of the Gonzaga family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After losing the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio</a> in 1371, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feltrino-gonzaga">Feltrino Gonzaga</a>, of the family that ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mantua">Mantua</a>, kept the small territory of Novellara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagnolo-in-piano">Bagnolo</a> in the plain north of Reggio. His descendants ruled it as an imperial fief, later recognised as a county.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts of Novellara governed a few villages and a fortified town from their castle, the Rocca. They lived from farming the rich plain and from service at larger courts, and maintained a modest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> court; the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelio-orsi">Lelio Orsi</a> worked for them in the 16th century. The state survived by careful loyalty to the emperors and good relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga</a>-Novellara died out in 1728. After disputes over the succession, the emperor granted the county to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rinaldo-d-este-duke-of-modena">Rinaldo d'Este</a> in 1737, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-novellara.jpg" alt="County of Novellara" width="320" height="487"><figcaption>Image: stamperia Ferrario · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carta_contea_Novellara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1371 CE – 1737 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Novellara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Feltrino Gonzaga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Emilian, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Novellara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1554&amp;focus=county-of-novellara" aria-label="County of Novellara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4936 593 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Novellara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5256 849L5229 694M5256 849L5261 855"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5261 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5256 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5256 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Novellara. <a href="/atlas?year=1554&amp;focus=county-of-novellara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
